# Project Cape HIIP Validation and Resource Classification

**Overview:** This analysis evaluates GEOPHIRES' HIP-RA-X volumetric heat-in-place tool against the
DeGolyer and MacNaughton (D&M) Heat Initially In Place (HIIP)
[report](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1853868/000162828026025821/exhibit991-sx1.htm)
prepared for Fervo Energy's Cape Station and filed with the SEC in June 2024, and then carries the
resource estimate through the steps that report leaves open.

Parameterised to match the filing's assumptions, HIP-RA-X reproduces its volumetric results closely.
The deterministic case agrees with the Low Estimate to within 0.5%, and a Monte Carlo simulation
centred on the temperature implied by the filing's own per-interval figures agrees with the Mean
Estimate to within 1%.

Beyond that agreement, the analysis supplies three things the report defers or places outside its
scope. It performs the heat recovery factor assessment D&M record as still required, which reduces
the un-risked capacity figure of 14,005 MW to {{ rec_elec_mw }} MW producible. It classifies the
resulting recoverable quantity under the Geothermal Resource Management System as
{{ rec_cumulative_category }} — the kind of designation the report states heat in place alone cannot
support. And it sets that resource beside a development GEOPHIRES models in full, which allows a
statement of economic potential in standard terms.

**Disclaimer: Independent Analysis:** This is an independent evaluation developed by the author and contributors to the
GEOPHIRES open-source project. It is not affiliated with, sponsored by, or endorsed by Fervo Energy, DeGolyer and MacNaughton,
the SEC, or any other party.
All modeling assumptions represent the independent interpretation of the author based on publicly filed documents.

