### Classification under GRMS

**SEC HIIP Claim:** The report classifies the Project Cape Area accumulation as *discovered*, and
states that *"application of any risk factor to HIIP does not equate HIIP with reserves or contingent
resources"*. It applies no further classification: no reserves, contingent or prospective categories,
no 1P/2P/3P or 1C/2C/3C designations, and no reporting standard cited by name.

**HIP-RA-X Evaluation:** That caveat marks the boundary of what a volumetric in-place estimate can
claim. Heat in place is the parent quantity, and classification applies to quantities that are
recoverable — which requires the recovery assessment above. Having performed it, the estimate can be
placed within the Geothermal Resource Management System, the geothermal adaptation of SPE-PRMS.

HIP-RA-X reports the classification alongside the physical results:

<pre>
<b>      ***GRMS CLASSIFICATION***</b>
      Resource Class:                {{ rec_resource_class }}
      Estimate Scenario:             {{ rec_estimate_scenario }}
      Cumulative Category:           {{ rec_cumulative_category }}
</pre>

Contingent Resources because the accumulation is discovered and this analysis establishes a
recoverable quantity without determining commerciality; Reserves would require that determination.
The cumulative category is derived from the resource class and estimate scenario rather than
declared, so the Best Estimate of Contingent Resources is reported as 2C. The filing's Low, Best and
High estimates correspond to the GRMS Low, Best and High estimate scenarios, and hence to 1C, 2C
and 3C.

Two designations are deliberately absent. Project maturity sub-class and economic status describe the
project rather than the resource — whether development is pending, on hold or approved, and whether
the economics have been demonstrated — and those are determinations for an evaluator with access to
the project, not conclusions an independent analysis of a public filing can draw. HIP-RA-X reports
only the designations declared, which keeps the boundary between the model's results and the
evaluator's judgement visible in the output itself.

### Economic Potential

**SEC HIIP Claim:** The report does not address economics. Its scope is heat in place, and it
states that a heat recovery factor assessment is still required even to reach recoverable heat.
Cost, price and financing are outside it entirely.

**HIP-RA-X Evaluation:** A volumetric assessment cannot supply them either. The recoverable
estimate above describes an accumulation, and an accumulation has no levelised cost: cost attaches
to a development, with a well count, a plant, a schedule and a financing structure. This is the
same boundary the classification marks — Contingent Resources are recoverable but not demonstrated
commercial, and the demonstration is a project, not a better resource estimate.

GEOPHIRES models such a project. `Fervo_Project_Cape-5` in this repository is a full techno-economic
simulation of a development at Cape Station:

| | Modelled development | Accumulation (this analysis) |
| :--- | ---: | ---: |
| Net electricity | {{ fpc5_elec_mw }} MW | {{ rec_elec_mw }} MW recoverable |
| Production wells | {{ fpc5_production_wells }} | &mdash; |
| Total CAPEX | {{ fpc5_capex_musd }} MUSD | &mdash; |
| Capital intensity | {{ fpc5_capex_per_kw }} USD/kW | &mdash; |
| Electricity breakeven price | {{ fpc5_breakeven }} cents/kWh | &mdash; |
| GRMS classification | project-specific | {{ rec_cumulative_category }} |

The modelled development accounts for roughly {{ fpc5_fraction_pct }}% of the recoverable estimate.
The remainder is not a smaller or riskier version of the same thing: it is resource with no
development plan attached, and therefore no cost, no schedule and no commerciality determination.
At the project's {{ fpc5_mw_per_well }} MW per production well it would take several hundred further
wells, and the reservoir behaviour, drilling cost and offtake arrangements for those wells are
precisely what has not been established.

No extrapolation of the project's capital intensity across the full accumulation is offered here,
and none should be inferred. Capital intensity does not scale linearly over a sixfold expansion:
well interference, the temperature gradient across the accumulation, drilling learning curves and
transmission capacity all bear on it, and none of them are modelled by multiplication.

What the two figures jointly support, in standard terms, is this. The Project Cape Area holds a
best-estimate recoverable resource classified as {{ rec_cumulative_category }}, of which a
development modelled at {{ fpc5_elec_mw }} MW and {{ fpc5_breakeven }} cents/kWh demonstrates
commercial viability for roughly {{ fpc5_fraction_pct }}%. Converting further {{ rec_cumulative_category }}
volumes to Reserves requires development plans, not further resource assessment. That is a statement
about economic potential that neither the filing nor a volumetric tool could make alone.

**Comparability:** `Fervo_Project_Cape-5` models a specific stimulated volume with its own reservoir
parameterisation, calibrated against published Cape Station performance. The analysis above models
the whole accumulation from the filing's volumetric figures. The two are set side by side here on
scale and maturity; they are not reconciled as models of the same object, and the difference between
their capacity figures should not be read as a disagreement.

